course: dictionary
lesson: 2125
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one is right. Conviction is one of these words. «She has a deep conviction that animals should be treated kindly» means she strongly believes it.
explain: The Water Rat says, «Yes, it's the life, the only life, to live», but he does it «without his usual whole-hearted conviction». Here conviction is not a belief. It is the feeling of being sure, shown in how you speak. Without it, he sounds less sure than usual.
explain: In a court, conviction means the decision that someone is guilty, as in «a conviction for theft». Read the words around it: a belief, a way of speaking, or a court case. Then swap in your meaning to check that it fits.
fact: Conviction means a strong belief or opinion.
fact: Conviction can also mean the feeling of being sure, as in «He spoke with conviction».
fact: In law, a conviction is a court's decision that someone is guilty of a crime.
fact: «Previous convictions» means times someone was found guilty before.
fact: The opposite of a conviction in a court is an acquittal, which means found not guilty.
exercise: «She has a deep conviction that animals should be treated kindly.» What does conviction mean here? | A small cut on the skin | *A strong belief | A race won by running fast | A bill to pay
exercise: «He spoke with conviction.» What does conviction mean here? | A list of rules | A loud noise | Being very tired | *The feeling of being sure
exercise: In a court, what is the opposite of a conviction? | *An acquittal | A holiday | A rehearsal | A prize
test: «without his usual whole-hearted conviction» What does conviction mean in this sentence? | A long sleep | A type of boat | A way of walking slowly | *The feeling of being sure
test: «He had no previous convictions.» What does this mean? | He had never met anyone before | *He had never been found guilty before | He had never had a birthday | He had never believed in anything
exam: Ella says conviction always means a court case. Is she right? | *No, it can also mean a strong belief or the feeling of being sure | Yes, but only in speaking lessons | No, it means a type of food | Yes, it always means a court case
